facts:
$y=kf(x)$ is a **vertical** dilation of factor $k$ from the $x$-axis, so $(x,y)\to(x,ky)$ and every $y$-coordinate is multiplied by $k$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-3.6,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.6) -- (0,3.9) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,gray,dashed,<->,domain=-2.9:2.9,smooth,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-0.5*\x*\x});
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-2.5:2.5,smooth,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{3-0.75*\x*\x});
\node[blue,right] at (-3.5,3.2) {$y=kf(x)$};
\node[gray,right] at (-3.5,2.3) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

$y=f(ax)$ is a **horizontal** dilation of factor $\dfrac{1}{a}$ from the $y$-axis, so $(x,y)\to\left(\dfrac{x}{a},y\right)$.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}]
\draw[->] (-3.6,0) -- (3.6,0) node[right] {$x$};
\draw[->] (0,-2.2) -- (0,3.3) node[above] {$y$};
\draw[thick,gray,dashed,<->,domain=-2.7:2.7,smooth,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-0.5*\x*\x});
\draw[thick,blue,<->,domain=-1.35:1.35,smooth,samples=61,variable=\x] plot ({\x},{2-2*\x*\x});
\node[blue,right] at (-3.5,2.9) {$y=f(ax)$};
\node[gray,right] at (-3.5,2.0) {$y=f(x)$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

